 ## Workspace and Session lists
 
+### Addressed Web boot
+
+The Web client accepts an opt-in generic addressed presentation through `?embedded=1&session=<root-session-id>`. Boot parameters are parsed before `SessionRuntime` is constructed, so an addressed runtime never restores or briefly projects the process-wide `dsh.sessions.current` selection. It uses an in-memory selection initialized to the requested root instead; the first successful Host `session.list` baseline must contain that exact root before the session-scoped shell can mount. A missing root therefore fails closed without falling back to another listed Session.
+
+`expectedSession` is an optional accidental-carrier-mismatch check: when present it must exactly equal `session`. It is not an authorization source. Repeated reserved parameters, empty or malformed ids, and embedded presentation without `session` reject Web boot. Addressed mode may also run with the ordinary global presentation by supplying `session` without `embedded=1`. Unrecognized display parameters are ignored and can never grant Session, tool, or Agent authority.
+
+Addressed navigation derives every switch from the current root-reachable Host catalog graph; visiting a child never creates durable authority. A successful `subagent.list` refresh is the membership baseline. While a later refresh is `loading`, its last-success entries remain the most recent Host-proven evidence and navigation may continue against that exact graph. A refresh error contributes no edges, and a successful refresh that removes or changes an edge immediately revokes that descendant and everything below it. The root-reachable graph must be structurally valid and acyclic; corrupt or cyclic evidence fails closed. `open()`, `openSubagent()`, return-to-root, automatic catalog refresh, and reload all apply the same rule, preventing stale descendants, siblings, and cross-root selection. Standard Web boot keeps the existing persisted-current and navigation behavior unchanged.
+
+An addressed runtime is also a generic fixed-root browser capability: `session.create`, `session.fork`, Workspace connect/create, and New Session flows fail before Host mutation. Presentation plugins disable or hide their create/fork controls, while the service guard remains the final side-effect fence. Existing root and catalog-proven subagent transcripts, details, continuation, and return navigation remain available.
+
 Workspace and Session lists have independent monotone `pending` → `ready` baseline phases and separate refresh activity/error state. Incremental upsert/removal/order frames and unary mutation echoes arriving during a list request replay over its response. Every successful Workspace baseline re-establishes Host-durable Workspace order so reconnects adopt changes committed while this client was offline. `WorkspaceRuntime.insertBefore` installs an optimistic order immediately; only the latest unary echo may replace it, a newer Host order frame outranks an older echo, and a latest rejected request restores the last Host-confirmed order rather than an earlier uncommitted drag. Removed Workspace ids retain process-local tombstones so late changed frames cannot resurrect them. Workspace recency is derived only after both baselines are ready and never changes Workspace list order.
 
 `SessionSummary.pendingInteraction` classifies the live user action blocking a Session as `approval`, `plan-review`, or `question`. `SessionManager` tracks answerable requested/resolved mux frames by their stable request identities even before a Session object is instantiated; pre-instantiation buffering retains every live request, replaces replay duplicates, and removes resolved requests so the list status always has a matching answerable `PendingWait` when the Session is opened. The first pending question takes presentation priority over concurrent approvals to match composer routing, while only a request that satisfies the plan-review composer's binary rendering constraints keeps the distinct `plan-review` status. The state is connection-generation scoped: disconnect clears it, and mux-open replay restores only requests that remain pending.
